  "query_preview": "(Today through Saturday)...\nIssued at 303 AM EDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nTHROUGH TONIGHT...\n\nOne or more upper level waves, at least partially convectively-\nenhanced, will traverse the area this morning into tonight, with \nattendant threats for additional showers and thunderstorms. Modest \nbut sufficient deep layer shear will remain in place, along with \nample instability in the continued warm and very moist environment. \nAreas of low level convergence associated both with surface \nboundaries and remnant convective outflows will serve as foci for \ndevelopment, but given the complex situation and CAM struggles with \nplacement and consistency run-to-run, confidence is lowered. \nLocalized damaging wind gusts will remain the primary severe threat.\n\nNeedless to say, flash flood concerns will remain the most pressing \nworry, however. HREF local probability matched means suggest a \nthreat for up to a 1-2+ inches of rain with the most intense storms, \nwhich will continue to pose a extremely rapid onset flash flood \nthreat, though there is some guidance suggestion that the heavier \nrainfall threat may be more central and south, which, while still \nquite wet, is not quite in as rough a shape as areas from Indy metro \nnorth and northeast.\n\nMONDAY AND TUESDAY...\n\nAn area of surface high pressure will mercifully bring at least a \ntemporary end to the repeated rounds of thunderstorms early in the \nweek, with dry weather and near normal temperatures likely early \nMonday into Tuesday.\n\nTUESDAY NIGHT INTO WEDNESDAY NIGHT...\n\nUnfortunately, an additional round of thunderstorms appears likely \nlate Tuesday night into Wednesday night as another cold front and \nupper level wave accompanying a strong jet streak aloft passes \nthrough the region. Plentiful moisture will be available, though \nprecipitable water values may be slightly more modest (70-80th \npercentile climatologically). However, somewhat stronger deep layer \nshear may be available. Another round of severe storms will \ncertainly be possible, though predictability is greatly limited \ngiven the sensitivity to prior rounds of storms and model \ninconsistencies 4 days out.\n\nThat said, even with a couple of days to dry out, flash flood \nvulnerability will remain quite high in the face of any additional \nconvective rainfall given the extreme antecedent conditions, and we \nwill need to remain vigilant through at least mid week for these \nconcerns.\n\nTHURSDAY ONWARD...\n\nAmplification of the upper ridge over the western CONUS and \nsouthward displacement of both the frontal zone and deeper moisture \nby surface high pressure and upper level troughing suggest milder \ntemperatures and mostly dry conditions late week and into the \nweekend, though some slight chances for showers or storms may linger \nacross the southern portions of central Indiana. It will take quite \nsome time for antecedent conditions to significantly improve, \nunfortunately, and any additional convective rainfall with periodic \nfrontal passages or upper level waves in the broader cyclonic flow \nnext weekend and into the following week will pose at least modest \nflash flood threats.",
